Understanding all three patterns lets you choose the right one for each application type.

**Synchronizer Token (server-side storage)**: Spring Security generates a token per session. Thymeleaf templates automatically include it as a hidden field: `<input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="...">`. For SPAs making AJAX requests, the token is available at `/api/csrf` or as a meta tag, and the frontend must include it as the `X-CSRF-TOKEN` header.

**Double Submit Cookie (`CookieCsrfTokenRepository`)**: Spring Security sets the token in a cookie that JavaScript can read (not HttpOnly). The SPA reads the cookie and sends the value as the `X-XSRF-TOKEN` header. Angular's `HttpClientXsrfModule` and Axios both support this automatically. For SPAs, this is the standard choice — no extra API call needed, the cookie is always available.

```java
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        .csrf(csrf -> csrf
            .csrfTokenRepository(CookieCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse())) // SPA pattern
        .build();
}
```

**`SameSite` cookie attribute** is a browser-level CSRF defense that requires no application-layer tokens. `SameSite=Lax` (the browser default since Chrome 80) blocks cookies on cross-site POST requests and AJAX — covering the most dangerous CSRF attack vectors. `SameSite=Strict` blocks cookies on all cross-site requests, including link navigation. Configure this for Spring Boot session cookies: `server.servlet.session.cookie.same-site=lax`. When `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict` is set, CSRF token protection becomes redundant for modern browsers — you may disable the token mechanism if IE support is not required.

The `CorsConfigurationSource` bean is the single source of truth for what cross-origin requests the server accepts:

```java
@Bean
public CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
    CorsConfiguration config = new CorsConfiguration();
    config.setAllowedOrigins(List.of(
        "https://app.example.com",
        "https://admin.example.com"
    ));
    config.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS"));
    config.setAllowedHeaders(List.of("Authorization", "Content-Type", "X-Requested-With"));
    config.setAllowCredentials(true);  // required for cookies or Authorization header
    config.setMaxAge(3600L);           // cache preflight for 1 hour — reduces OPTIONS requests

    UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource source = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
    source.registerCorsConfiguration("/**", config);
    return source;
}
```

**The `allowCredentials(true)` + wildcard origin incompatibility**: setting `allowCredentials(true)` and `allowedOrigins("*")` simultaneously is rejected by browsers. When credentials are required, the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` response header must be a specific origin (not `*`) — browsers enforce this. List specific origins explicitly.

**`setAllowedOriginPatterns("*")`** is different from `setAllowedOrigins("*")`. The pattern-based variant substitutes the actual request origin in the response header dynamically instead of sending a literal `*`. This works with `allowCredentials(true)`. Use this only if you genuinely cannot enumerate origins at configuration time — for production APIs, enumerating specific origins is strongly preferred.

**`maxAge(3600)`** instructs browsers to cache the preflight response for one hour. Without this, every non-simple cross-origin request generates two HTTP round-trips — the preflight OPTIONS followed by the actual request. For high-traffic APIs with many cross-origin calls, this caching significantly reduces overhead.

CORS configuration should differ by environment — what's appropriate for development is a security risk in production.

For environment-specific origins, inject the allowed origins from configuration:

```java
@Value("${cors.allowed-origins}")
private List<String> allowedOrigins;

@Bean
public CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
    CorsConfiguration config = new CorsConfiguration();
    config.setAllowedOrigins(allowedOrigins); // from application.yml per environment
    // ... rest of config
}
```

In `application.yml`: `cors.allowed-origins: http://localhost:3000`
In `application-prod.yml`: `cors.allowed-origins: https://app.example.com,https://admin.example.com`

**Never use wildcard `*` in production** when credentials are involved. A wildcard origin with credentials is a misconfiguration that browsers reject anyway — but it's also a security signal that the CORS policy wasn't designed carefully. Enumerate specific origins.

**Debugging CORS errors**: Use `curl -X OPTIONS https://api.example.com/endpoint -H "Origin: https://app.example.com" -v` to inspect exactly what response headers the server returns for a preflight. The response should include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, `Access-Control-Allow-Methods`, and `Access-Control-Allow-Headers`. If any of these are missing or incorrect, the browser blocks the actual request.

**For microservice architectures**, configure CORS at the API gateway (Spring Cloud Gateway, Kong, AWS API Gateway) and disable or relax it in individual services. Services behind the gateway receive requests only from the gateway — which is on the same domain — so cross-origin configuration in each service adds nothing except complexity and maintenance overhead.

A complete CORS configuration in Spring Security uses `CorsConfigurationSource` registered with the `SecurityFilterChain`. This is the correct approach — not `@CrossOrigin` on controllers (which Spring Security's filter chain ignores) and not `WebMvcConfigurer.addCorsMappings()` alone (which doesn't apply to OPTIONS preflight requests intercepted by Spring Security).

```java
@Bean
CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
    CorsConfiguration config = new CorsConfiguration();
    config.setAllowedOrigins(List.of("https://app.example.com"));  // explicit origins
    config.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS"));
    config.setAllowedHeaders(List.of("Authorization", "Content-Type"));
    config.setAllowCredentials(true);   // required for cookie-based auth
    config.setMaxAge(3600L);            // cache preflight for 1 hour
    UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource source = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
    source.registerCorsConfiguration("/**", config);
    return source;
}
```

**`setAllowCredentials(true)` is incompatible with wildcard origins.** Setting both `allowedOrigins = ["*"]` and `allowCredentials = true` throws an error — the CORS spec prohibits this combination. With credentials enabled (cookies, Authorization headers), the server must reflect the exact request origin, not `*`. The browser enforces this: a cross-origin request with credentials is rejected if the response headers contain `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.

**Preflight requests** (HTTP OPTIONS) must be permitted without authentication. `permitAll()` for `OPTIONS` requests prevents Spring Security from blocking preflight. Without this, the browser's preflight receives 401, the main request never fires, and the developer sees an opaque CORS error.

When Spring Security is in the picture, CORS configuration must happen at the security filter chain level — not just in `WebMvcConfigurer`. A preflight OPTIONS request rejected by Spring Security never reaches your MVC CORS config.

**The layering problem**
- Request arrives → Spring Security filter chain → `DispatcherServlet` → Spring MVC
- A CORS preflight (OPTIONS) rejected at the filter chain level returns a `403` before Spring MVC sees it
- `WebMvcConfigurer.addCorsMappings()` alone is not enough when Spring Security is present

**Correct configuration**
```java
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http, CorsConfigurationSource corsSource) throws Exception {
    http.cors(cors -> cors.configurationSource(corsSource));
    // ... other config
    return http.build();
}

@Bean
public CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
    CorsConfiguration config = new CorsConfiguration();
    config.setAllowedOrigins(List.of("https://app.example.com"));
    config.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS"));
    config.setAllowCredentials(true);
    UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource source = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
    source.registerCorsConfiguration("/**", config);
    return source;
}
```

**The wildcard + credentials rule**
- `allowedOrigins("*")` with `allowCredentials(true)` violates the CORS spec — browsers reject it
- Use `allowedOriginPatterns("https://*.example.com")` to allow subdomains with credentials
